    <title>
        feat: embedded arcs with the homotopy extension property through fini…
    </title>
    <updated>2026-11-06T22:42:13Z</updated>
    <media:thumbnail height="30" width="30" url="https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/339378563?s=30&amp;v=4"/>
    <author>
      <name>tauceti-ai-for-science</name>
      <uri>https://github.com/tauceti-ai-for-science</uri>
    </author>
    <content type="html">
      &lt;pre style=&#39;white-space:pre-wrap;width:81ex&#39;&gt;feat: embedded arcs with the homotopy extension property through finite sets of a manifold (#29671)

This PR proves that in a nonempty preconnected Hausdorff `C^n` manifold `M`, `n ≥ 2`, modelled on a finite-dimensional real normed space of dimension at least two, every finite set `S ⊆ M` lies on an embedded arc `γ : [0, 1] → M` whose image is contractible and has the homotopy extension property (`TauCeti.exists_isClosedEmbedding_hasHomotopyExtensionProperty_range`). This image is an embedded tree joining the points of `S`, and it is exactly the input of the collapse theorem `TauCeti.HasHomotopyExtensionProperty.collapseHomotopyEquiv`, so `M → M / γ(I)` is a homotopy equivalence with a specified homotopy inverse.

Roadmap target: `DGFloer/README.md`, Layer 5 (&amp;quot;Morse homology with DG coefficients&amp;quot;), item 2, **The Barraud–Cornea cocycle**, first bullet: &amp;quot;Choose an embedded tree joining the critical points to the basepoint, and a homotopy inverse of the collapse map.&amp;quot; The homotopy inverse of the collapse is already on main (https://github.com/TauCetiProject/TauCeti/pull/27897 &amp;quot;feat: collapsing a contractible subspace is a homotopy equivalence&amp;quot;), whose description records that the bullet &amp;quot;still needs the embedded tree itself, meaning a contractible closed subspace with the homotopy extension property that contains the critical points and the basepoint&amp;quot;. This PR supplies that tree, with `S` the critical points together with the basepoint. After this PR the bullet still lacks the case of a base of dimension at most one (the circle and the line), which the diffeotopy argument here does not cover; the rest of item 2 (evaluating the representing chains into Moore paths and then into `C_*(ΩB)`) waits on the representing chain systems of item 1.

Two new files:
- `TauCeti/Topology/Homotopy/Extension/Segment.lean` (250 lines): `OpenPartialHomeomorph.hasHomotopyExtensionProperty_symm_image_segment`. If a segment `[x, y]` lies in the target of an open partial homeomorphism `e` from a Hausdorff space to a proper real normed space, then `e.symm &amp;#39;&amp;#39; [x, y]` has the homotopy extension property. The NDR data comes from `TauCeti.NDRPair.ofHomotopyRel`. The retraction is the projection onto the segment along the kernel of a linear form `ℓ` with `ℓ (y - x) = 1` (from Mathlib&amp;#39;s `SeparatingDual.exists_eq_one`). The deformation is the straight-line homotopy inside a convex thickening of the segment. The control function is the rescaled distance to the projection, extended by `1` off the chart.
- `TauCeti/Geometry/Manifold/EmbeddedArc.lean` (140 lines): the main theorem. A short straight segment in a chart gives an arc `α` with the homotopy extension property. The existing configuration theorem `TauCeti.exists_diffeotopy_final_comp_eq_of_isPreconnected` moves the points of `S` onto distinct points of `α` by a diffeomorphism `Ψ`, and the arc is `Ψ⁻¹ ∘ α`; the homotopy extension property transports by `TauCeti.HasHomotopyExtensionProperty.image`.

References cited in the files: Steenrod&amp;#39;s NDR pairs; Barraud–Cornea, *Lagrangian intersections and the Serre spectral sequence*; Barraud–Damian–Humilière–Oancea, *Morse homology with differential graded coefficients*; Milnor&amp;#39;s homogeneity lemma. No Mathlib PR material or external formalization is vendored.

Roadmap: DGFloer

&amp;lt;!--tauceti-target:v1 {&amp;quot;focus&amp;quot;:&amp;quot;DGFloer&amp;quot;,&amp;quot;id&amp;quot;:&amp;quot;embedded-tree-joining-critical-points-to-basepoint&amp;quot;}--&amp;gt;

🤖 Prepared with Claude Code

