  <entry>
    <id>tag:github.com,2008:Grit::Commit/5990523b6f840c76b469885708d3c8a57f5b2b50</id>
    <link type="text/html" rel="alternate" href="https://github.com/TauCetiProject/TauCeti/commit/5990523b6f840c76b469885708d3c8a57f5b2b50"/>
    <title>
        feat: compute curvature of induced Hom connections (#30704)
    </title>
    <updated>2026-11-06T22:42:13Z</updated>
    <media:thumbnail height="30" width="30" url="https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/32490532?s=30&amp;v=4"/>
    <author>
      <name>riccardobrasca</name>
      <uri>https://github.com/riccardobrasca</uri>
    </author>
    <content type="html">
      &lt;pre style=&#39;white-space:pre-wrap;width:81ex&#39;&gt;feat: compute curvature of induced Hom connections (#30704)

This PR computes the curvature of the native induced Hom connection. Establish `Rᴴ(X,Y)A = Rᵂ(X,Y) ∘ A - A ∘ Rⱽ(X,Y)` on smooth sections and on arbitrary fibre vectors; in particular, identify the curvature of the induced endomorphism connection with the commutator action of the original curvature.

Advance [RiemannianGeometry/README.md, milestone R1, item 1, “Intrinsic curvature calculus and normal-coordinate jets”](https://github.com/TauCetiProject/TauCetiRoadmap/blob/main/TauCetiRoadmap/RiemannianGeometry/README.md#r1-intrinsic-curvature-calculus-and-normal-coordinate-jets), specifically its End-valued curvature calculus and `(d_∇)²=R∧·` requirement. The End(V)-valued specialization of that statement consumes `CovariantDerivative.curvatureTensor_hom_apply cov cov` to identify the actual coefficient-connection curvature with `[R,A]`. This supplies the missing induced-curvature calculation, rather than another coefficient bundle or connection. After this PR, R1.1 still needs the exterior-covariant derivative comparisons and full form-level structure/Bianchi identities; the remaining normal-coordinate jets and other R1 geometric constructions also remain.

Choose this independent algebraic prerequisite because main already supplies `CovariantDerivative.hom`, its smoothness and evaluation rule, and the pointwise curvature tensor, but no Hom-curvature formula. Check the requested open and recent merged PR lists, recent mainline history and focused all-age Hom/curvature searches. The open induced-∇R identification (#30680), metric jet (#30577), fourth Jacobi derivative (#30633), warped Ricci formula (#28936), flatness (#27690) and submersion projection (#28606) work supplies different statements. Import only merged mathematics. Cooperative acquisition of `author/RiemannianGeometry/curvaturetensor-hom` returns the documented exit-2 permission fallback.

Add `TauCeti/Geometry/Manifold/VectorBundle/CovariantDerivative/Curvature/Hom.lean` (128 lines), with two public theorems and one private calculation. The section-level formula allows an arbitrary-rank target bundle; the pointwise tensor formula uses finite-rank source and target bundles, matching the existing curvature-tensor API. Both allow manifold boundary and need no metric, torsion, completeness, connectedness or positive-dimension hypothesis. Reuse the native Hom connection, curvature operator, tensor evaluation law and smooth section extensions. Cite Lee, *Introduction to Riemannian Manifolds*, second edition, Chapters 4 and 7, in the module. No Mathlib infrastructure or external formalization is vendored.

Read the shared protocol and all ten rubrics: scope, correctness, reuse, attribution, api-design, generality, placement, naming, documentation and proof-quality. Self-review repeats pinned-Mathlib and Tau Ceti searches for the added statements, checks the source-curvature minus sign and zero-rank cases, removes an unused finite-rank target assumption from the section calculation, and marks the pointwise evaluation formula as a simplification rule. Keep the public API to the section and fibre formulas, without adding a redundant endomorphism specialization.

Local foreground verification passes Mathlib cache retrieval, the public Tau Ceti cache retrieval, plain whole-library `lake build --iofail` (68,716 jobs), whitespace checking, the changed-module axiom audit and all fourteen changed-module environment linters. These are local checks, not CI; CI verifies the pushed PR, including transitive axioms and global ownership checks.

Local changed-file checks:

```text
