    <id>tag:github.com,2008:Grit::Commit/cb53793219d9262f53951db8ee85b3d58adec517</id>
    <link type="text/html" rel="alternate" href="https://github.com/TauCetiProject/TauCeti/commit/cb53793219d9262f53951db8ee85b3d58adec517"/>
    <title>
        feat: stabilize Fredholm families inside dense subspaces with exact c…
    </title>
    <updated>2026-11-06T22:42:13Z</updated>
    <media:thumbnail height="30" width="30" url="https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/339378563?s=30&amp;v=4"/>
    <author>
      <name>tauceti-ai-for-science</name>
      <uri>https://github.com/tauceti-ai-for-science</uri>
    </author>
    <content type="html">
      &lt;pre style=&#39;white-space:pre-wrap;width:81ex&#39;&gt;feat: stabilize Fredholm families inside dense subspaces with exact cokernel projections (#29839)

This PR lets the finite stabilizer of a Fredholm family be chosen inside any dense subspace of the target. It also shows that wherever the cokernel keeps its base-point dimension, the stabilizer is an exact topological complement of the range. The projection onto the stabilizer along the range is a component of the right inverse of the stabilized operator normalized by the kernel coordinate, so it is continuous, or `C^n`, with the family.

Roadmap: HeegaardFloer

Roadmap target: `HeegaardFloer/README.md`, §3.4 &amp;quot;Maslov and Conley–Zehnder conventions and determinant lines (F1.4)&amp;quot;, paragraph **Stabilized linear gluing**. That paragraph asks for &amp;quot;a finite-dimensional stabilizer Sᵢ in its target, represented by smooth compactly supported sections and projecting isomorphically to coker Dᵢ. Construct these representatives using density and the native Fredholm splitting.&amp;quot; It also asks, for a continuous or `C^k` operator family, for &amp;quot;the exact-coker stabilizer subbundles on constant-kernel/cokernel-rank parameter ranges, with their correspondingly regular splittings&amp;quot;. The consumer is the augmented operator `D_R ⊕ j_R : X_R ⊕ (S₁⊕S₂) → Y_R` of that paragraph. Its stabilizers must be smooth compactly supported so that they can be spliced into the glued target. Here they come from `IsFredholm.exists_stabilization_kernel_coordinates` with `D` the dense subspace of such sections. After this PR, F1.4&amp;#39;s stabilized gluing still needs F1.1&amp;#39;s cylindrical Sobolev domains, the splicing and uniformly bounded right inverse of `D_R ⊕ j_R`, and the exact sequence `0 → ker D_R → ker D₁⊕ker D₂ → S₁⊕S₂ → coker D_R → 0` with its determinant comparison.

Changes:
- `TauCeti/Analysis/Fredholm/Stabilization.lean`: `ContinuousLinearMap.IsFredholm.exists_stabilization_kernel_coordinates` now takes a dense submodule `D` of the target and returns a stabilizer `S ≤ D` with `IsTopCompl S (T x₀).range`. The stabilizer comes from the existing `Submodule.exists_le_isTopCompl_of_dense` instead of a Fredholm package.
- `TauCeti/Analysis/Fredholm/KernelBundle.lean`: `IsFredholm.exists_stabilization_kernel_vectorBundle` takes the same dense subspace and returns `S ≤ D`.
- `TauCeti/Analysis/Normed/Operator/Splitting/Basic.lean`: `ContinuousLinearMap.isInvertible_prod_comm`, which says `(A.prod q).IsInvertible ↔ (q.prod A).IsInvertible`. Read with the roles exchanged, `q.kerSection A` is the right inverse of `A` with values in `ker q`, so the existing `kerSection` API and its regularity theorem `contDiffAt_kerSection` apply to right inverses unchanged. New, for a stabilizer `V` and a coordinate `q` whose pairing with `T + inclusion_V` is invertible, over topological modules:
  - `apply_fst_kerSection_coprod_add_snd` (`simp`): the normalized right inverse `q.kerSection (T.coprod V.subtypeL)` splits each `y` as `T e + v`.
  - `snd_kerSection_coprod_apply` (`simp`): its `V`-component is `V.projectionOnto T.range` when `V` and the range are complementary.
  - `isTopCompl_of_isInvertible_prod_coprod`: over a division ring, if `finrank V ≤ finrank (F ⧸ T.range)` (equivalently, the cokernel has the dimension of `V`), then `V` is a topological complement of the range.
  - `projectionOntoL_eq_snd_comp_kerSection_coprod`: the continuous projection onto `V` along the range, as `snd ∘L q.kerSection (T.coprod V.subtypeL)`.
- `TauCeti/Analysis/Normed/Operator/Splitting/Family.lean`: `continuousAt_snd_comp_kerSection_coprod` and `contDiffAt_snd_comp_kerSection_coprod`: that component is continuous, or `C^n`, wherever the family is and the pairing is invertible.
- `TauCeti/LinearAlgebra/Dimension/Submodule.lean`: `Submodule.isCompl_of_codisjoint_of_finrank_le`. A finite-dimensional `S` with `V ⊔ S = ⊤` and `finrank S ≤ finrank (E ⧸ V)` is a complement of `V`, with `V` possibly infinite-dimensional.

The rank hypothesis cannot be dropped. For `T_t : ℝ → ℝ, x ↦ t x` stabilized by `V = ℝ`, the stabilized operator is surjective for every `t`, but `V` meets the range for `t ≠ 0`. This is the rank jump of the F1.4 check.

I searched the pinned Mathlib for complements from codisjointness and a dimension bound (`IsCompl`, `Codisjoint`, `finrank`, `map_mkQ_eq_top`) and for invertibility of swapped pairs (`prodComm`, `IsInvertible`), and found neither. The source is McDuff–Salamon, *J-holomorphic Curves and Symplectic Topology*, Appendix A.2, already cited in the file.

&amp;lt;!--tauceti-target:v1 {&amp;quot;focus&amp;quot;:&amp;quot;HeegaardFloer&amp;quot;,&amp;quot;id&amp;quot;:&amp;quot;fredholm-stabilizer-in-dense-subspace&amp;quot;}--&amp;gt;

